cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or manuscript, please cite it as below."
title: "Inner-Horizon Stability of the Hayward Metric: Closure Classes in the Ori Model, the Evaporation Clock and a Nonlinear Double-Null Test"
abstract: "Does the inner (Cauchy) horizon of a regular Hayward black hole survive perturbation, or does mass inflation destroy it? In the Ori thin-null-shell model, reconstructed for an arbitrary closure M(m,r) and checked against Reissner-Nordstrom, the family a(m) ~ m^(1+s) has three regimes: unbounded exponential growth for -1 <= s < 0; for the fixed-length closure s = 0, exponential growth followed by M_+ ~ v^(p+1), but only because the Moebius map is continued through m = infinity; and for s > 0 a fold that ends the model at finite advanced time. With the evaporation law of Paper A the integrated inner-horizon exponent of a 10 solar-mass hole with l = l_P is about 10^121 e-folds and diverges near the critical mass. In a nonlinear double-null evolution of the Einstein-NED-massless-scalar system with the additive closure M = mu + M_N(r), the Cauchy-horizon radius stays finite below a critical amplitude while the Misner-Sharp mass inflates; at the threshold the radius vanishes as (A_* - A)^gamma with gamma = 0.4997 +- 0.002. The sign of the inflating mass is that of -r_u, and the conformal-anomaly term of the Hawking flux, added along a ray, reverses it. The Kretschmann scalar obeys K >= 16 M^2/r^6, which diverges along the rays computed to v = 280. The closure of the nonlinear test is not the mass-tracking closure of the series, and extendibility is addressed by indicators only. The archive contains the LaTeX source, the compiled PDF, and the Python and C code, logs and stored data that reproduce every table, number and figure."
